00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.410
AMY BANZAERT: All right,
so those are all really

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.420
great presentations.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.570
Thanks, you guys.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.910
I'm excited about all the
projects you learned about

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.450
and things you accomplished.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.510
So now, as I mentioned
at the beginning, what

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.380
we're going to do is very
quickly go through the design

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.640
process, and then talk about the
entire remainder of the class.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.640
And part of that will be
you guys down-selecting

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.570
from the projects you've
just discussed to the top few

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.130
that you think are
good candidates.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.050
And then tonight,
everyone in the class

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.510
will let me know
in a survey what

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.200
you want to actually work on.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.099
And then, you'll be assigned
into your final project groups

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:05.330
on Friday.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:09.710
So last year, some teams wanted
to work with the same community

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.540
partner, or all wanted
to work together,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.800
and other teams wanted to
do something different.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.900
So there's opportunity
for change or not change,

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.970
depending on what you want.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:26.270
So basically, doing design
and the design process

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.720
is challenging for some
pretty standard reasons of you

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.400
have these really
severe trade-offs

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.690
between you want something
that's really light but really

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.600
strong, et cetera.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.220
It can be a very
dynamic process.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.770
There's a lot of things
happening at once.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.712
There's a lot of
details to worry about.

00:01:44.712 --> 00:01:46.670
There's always time
pressure and there's always

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.990
economic pressure.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:52.370
And that's even more true
when you're doing design

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.990
for developing countries.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:02:00.680
So you need to know your user
and understand the need, which

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.750
hopefully, you've just addressed
in your trip but of course,

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.530
in a week you're not going
to truly fully understand

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:07.970
everything.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:09.470
There can be ethical
concerns that

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.510
come up that are
more severe than they

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.540
are at a typical
corporate design issue.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.840
And there are some pretty
extreme design constraints

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.340
in terms of the costs, and the
robustness, and is it usable.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.530
Can you manufacture
and distribute it?

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.563
And then also transparency--
is it easy to fix?

00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:30.230
Do people really
understand how it works

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.540
can be even more important.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.750
But it's all super rewarding,
too, so it's worth doing.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.680
And so there's a lot
of different ways

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.570
that people consider
the design process.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.610
So this is one way
of looking at it.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.360
So first, you gather
information, which you just

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.490
did, hypothetically, and
define the problem, which

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:54.840
you are doing or just did.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.810
And then you create
design specifications,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:01.430
which you're doing this week,
generating ideas, evaluating

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.303
your concepts, doing some
analysis and experimentation

00:03:05.303 --> 00:03:06.720
to really learn
what you're doing,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.040
doing detail design to
work out all the details,

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:12.090
fabricating, testing,
and evaluation.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.630
So that's one way
to think about it.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:22.130
Another way is to think
about it from more

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.760
of a staged standpoint.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.700
So you start with your
planning, and then you

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.660
get mission approval.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:31.040
So thinking about it that way,
Friday is mission approval.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.080
And then you do your
concept development,

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.110
and you have a concept
review, system-level design

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.960
with another review, detail
design and another review,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.190
testing and refinement.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:41.900
And then you're
approved for production

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.430
and can hypothetically
do production.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:49.940
Oh, there are some crazy
controls here that I did not

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:53.510
put in, but clearly came up.

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:57.290
So I tried to just map how
these two approaches work

00:03:57.290 --> 00:03:58.370
and how they connect.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.630
And of course, there's
actually other ways

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.970
of describing the
design process.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.080
And these all inter-link.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.323
And I'm not going into
these in a lot of detail.

00:04:08.323 --> 00:04:10.490
But we're going to just go
through this in the class

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.020
if you haven't seen it already.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.360
But another one to look at is
your functional requirements.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.089
So what does the
design have to do?

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:20.180
And then how is the design
going to meet the requirement?

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.327
Analyzing any decisions and
figuring out what you're doing,

00:04:24.327 --> 00:04:25.910
doing the research
to make sure you're

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:27.743
not building a better
mousetrap when there's

00:04:27.743 --> 00:04:29.630
plenty of good ones out there.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.520
The risk-- what's
going to happen

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:32.940
if things don't work out?

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.900
And then your countermeasures--
what if things don't work?

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.120
What do you do?

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.100
And this is from
Alex Slocum, and he

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:45.265
likes to use language
that I don't always use.

00:04:45.265 --> 00:04:46.640
This is from David
Wallace, who's

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.860
another Design Professor
of Mechanical Engineering.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.650
So all of these methods
I've talked about all

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.090
seem relatively
linear, which is a lie.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.790
So one way that
David likes to show

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.190
is that you're
always considering

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.860
your economic, your
physics, and your analysis.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.080
But you start out with a lot of
breadth and very little depth.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.140
And then as you go from
idea to concept to detail,

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.570
you get much more depth
and much less breadth.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:18.870
But you're always cycling
through all of the steps.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.660
So similarly, this is
another way of looking at it.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.890
So right now, we're going to be
doing Pugh charts to figure out

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.250
your projects.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.918
And then once you
figure your projects,

00:05:27.918 --> 00:05:29.960
you're going to do more
Pugh charts to figure out

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.280
what your implementation is.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.990
And then when you
figure that out, you're

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:34.865
going to get all the
way down to figuring out

00:05:34.865 --> 00:05:38.540
am I going to use
a screw or a bolt

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:41.390
with a nut to connect
these things, which screw

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:42.740
or nut and bolt?

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.568
And so each time,
you're winnowing down.

00:05:44.568 --> 00:05:46.610
And so you're repeating
a lot of the same things.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.170
You're going to have to
go back and do research.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:49.640
It's not like get
to do research once

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.432
at the beginning of
the class and you never

00:05:51.432 --> 00:05:52.940
have to investigate
anything again.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.800
It's just the level of
depth versus breadth

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.992
that you're considering.

00:05:56.992 --> 00:05:59.450
I talked about this a little
bit previously on the charcoal

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.070
project and how
the press evolved.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:06.770
But I just wanted to emphasize
that our class is, hopefully,

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.480
getting to a really
good prototype.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.040
And sometimes students have in
mind that's the end process.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:16.400
And that's not the best
way to think about things.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.010
If you think about,
say, an iPod,

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:22.550
it started out 10 years
ago with one iteration.

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.230
And that was a
production product

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:27.530
that had many prototypes
ahead of itself.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.160
And now, if you think 10 years
later, not only does the iPod

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:33.080
itself look really
different, but there's also

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:36.590
all these other implementations
of the iPod that are out there,

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:41.460
and many, many devices
along the way to get there.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:44.360
So that's just true.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.420
And iPod is an obviously
complex example,

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.020
but something even as simple
as a Netflix envelope in seven

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:54.170
years has gone through a
ridiculous number of design

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:55.310
changes and improvements.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.380
So it's just a reality
of the design process

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.980
that you're always getting
to the next, to a good stage,

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.475
hopefully, but you're
not ever getting to--

00:07:04.475 --> 00:07:07.100
you can't think that your first
prototype, the orange juice bag

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:08.225
stealer is a great example.

00:07:08.225 --> 00:07:09.710
You delivered it.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:11.720
It made sense a
year ago for being

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.070
checked to 12 volt battery.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:14.960
And now you can't find any,
and it has a different need,

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.377
and there's other
needs coming up.

00:07:16.377 --> 00:07:19.160
So there's always going
to be optimizations.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.160
OK, so I want to go
over a Pugh chart

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.950
today because that's what
you're going to be using.

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.540
If you haven't seen
it, Pugh was a guy

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:28.860
who came up with this method.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.750
And so it's just a way of
comparing different ideas.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.760
It seems, in some
ways that you do it,

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.860
it seems like it can be
kind of quantitative.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:37.970
And it's absolutely not.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.860
It's an entirely
qualitative method,

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.080
and using it quantitatively is
defeating the purpose of it.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.240
So the way you use it
is you have a bunch

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:52.950
of criteria on your
left vertical axis,

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.460
and then a bunch of
options that you compare.

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:00.620
I always pick the bad marker.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:02.220
A little better.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:10.760
And so today, we're just going
to compare a nail to a ring

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:21.840
shank nail to a
staple and a screw.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.022
So when you're
doing a Pugh chart,

00:08:24.022 --> 00:08:25.480
you're usually
doing it in a group.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.110
And so it's a good idea to
make sure that everyone knows

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:29.880
what each of these options are.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.740
So it can be helpful to
draw like a really fast,

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:36.120
simple diagram just so
that everyone, you're sure,

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.440
has in mind what
the differences are.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.860
So if you don't know,
a ring shank nail

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.409
is somewhere between
a screw and a nail,

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:45.400
and that it has little
rings on the edge

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:48.690
so that it helps prevent
the nail from popping

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:50.200
back out once you drive it in.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.200
But you still drive it
in like a nail as opposed

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.580
to screwing it in like a screw.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.880
And then a staple, obviously.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.840
So it's nice to have
a drawing, as well.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.860
And then you need
some criteria for how

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:08.930
you're going to compare
all of these options.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:19.810
And so you just go
through what you think

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.120
are the most important ones.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.070
There are more
complicated charts

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.670
that are versions of
Pugh charts, where

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:32.080
you can put different weighting
on your different criteria.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:33.910
That gets really hard
to do, especially

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:37.360
as you have more criteria,
like how much more important

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:38.580
is holding than effort?

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.580
It gets a little complicated
to figure that out.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.723
So sometimes, because
this is so qualitative,

00:09:42.723 --> 00:09:44.890
just having these out there
and then keeping in mind

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:48.080
your mental weightings,
is sufficient.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:54.100
And so then you want one of
your items to be your standard.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.290
So this is really
important, that you just

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.140
pick one that you think
might be the most standard,

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.900
and just give it an
S for standard, or 0,

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.200
or whatever you want to use.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.210
It's tempting not
to give a standard

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:08.530
because it's kind of annoying.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.300
It might mean that some--

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.460
like if the nail is
the cheapest option,

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.280
then your standard--
everything else

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.530
is going to be more expensive.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:17.080
Everything else is going
to be off the chart.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.630
So it's not actually in the
middle, which is what you want.

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:23.200
However, you always need
to compare to something

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.900
because there's no neutral.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:27.640
So you have to
define your neutral.

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:29.030
So this is the way they do that.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:31.300
So while it's tempting
to not define a neutral,

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.990
it makes the Pugh chart a whole
lot more useful if you do.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:38.140
And then you can just go
through and grade them,

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:40.617
so a ring shank
in terms of rate.

00:10:40.617 --> 00:10:41.950
And some people like to go down.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:43.540
Some people like to go across.

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:45.490
It's whatever you prefer.

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:51.400
So a ring shank is about the
same speed, a staple is faster.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.210
A screw is probably slower.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:56.050
So that's just how you do it.

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:59.530
For a cost, a ring shank is
going to be more expensive.

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.030
And that's another
confusing thing--

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:02.947
more expensive seems
like it should be a plus,

00:11:02.947 --> 00:11:04.940
but it's actually a minus
because that's worse.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.090
A staple will be
cheaper and a screw

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:09.400
is going to be more expensive.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.640
For holding, a ring
shank is a little better,

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:13.930
staple's a little worse.

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:15.190
A screw is a whole lot better.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.860
So you can do
minuses and pluses.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.060
You can do 1s and 2s.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:22.420
If you do 1s and 2s,
again, this is qualitative,

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:25.660
so no adding those
numbers at the end.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:29.365
And for effort, a
shank is similar.

00:11:29.365 --> 00:11:33.850
A staple is probably easier,
and a screw is harder.

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:36.190
So as I said, a lot
of people are then

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.580
tempted to put a line
at the bottom, and be,

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:41.200
like, this is a 0.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.592
This is a 0.

00:11:42.592 --> 00:11:51.120
This is [INAUDIBLE],,
which is not

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:52.560
the way to use a Pugh chart.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.240
So don't ever do this.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.900
And the reason is
that again, there's

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.350
different qualities,
different weightings for all

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:02.020
of these things.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.650
So adding these up
isn't that meaningful,

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.510
and isn't a good way
to make a decision.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:11.133
But it does very quickly
help you determine, just

00:12:11.133 --> 00:12:13.050
looking what are the
pros and cons of each one

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.720
in a visual way that's pretty
fast to really think about

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.837
the pros and cons, and
make sure you're actually

00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:20.170
considering the pros and cons
that are important to you.

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:24.060
And then finally, if we didn't
have the ring shank here,

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.300
and you saw the
nail and the screw,

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.370
the Pugh chart is
something that might

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:33.360
lead you to develop the ring
shank, where you're, like, OK,

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:36.150
the screw is slower,
and the cost is worse,

00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:39.010
and effort is worse, but
holding is really good.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:42.450
What could I do that would
combine my screw with my nail

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.240
to get me a better product?

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:50.280
And that might lead you to
develop the ring shank now.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.560
So that's one nice way
to use the Pugh chart is

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:56.610
to combine projects or
concepts, and help you do

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.050
that in a way that works well.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:59.925
Are there any questions
about the Pugh chart?

00:12:59.925 --> 00:13:03.710
Because you're going to
use it in 10 minutes.

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.260
OK.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:07.460
And this is just--

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:09.710
there it is.

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:12.578
Another tool that's common
that you might want to use

00:13:12.578 --> 00:13:14.120
is when you're
developing a schedule,

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:16.640
people often use something
called a Gantt chart.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:18.900
And can't read most
of what's on here.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:21.080
But basically,
you have your list

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:22.910
of tasks, how long
they're going to take,

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:24.208
and then you spread them out.

00:13:24.208 --> 00:13:26.750
And the thing that's important
or helpful about a Gantt chart

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.010
is listing the tasks
that rely on each other.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:32.390
So if one of your tasks is, say,
you're doing that rice husking

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.180
and you need a mill, and
you hadn't bought that mill,

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:36.950
you'd have to order a mill,
and it would have to be here.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:38.742
And you can't do any
testing with your mill

00:13:38.742 --> 00:13:39.650
until it gets here.

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:41.180
And so that's on
the critical path

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.250
because you need
to do the testing.

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:47.930
And therefore, that means you
need to order it yesterday.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:52.130
And it's a good thing you bought
it, so this is a helpful way

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.020
to think about your schedule.

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:55.520
It's not a requirement
in this class

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:56.750
that you use a Gantt
chart, so I'm not

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:57.950
going to talk about it anymore.

00:13:57.950 --> 00:13:59.990
But you can Google it and
find lots of different tools.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.157
But it's just one way of
building your schedule that

00:14:02.157 --> 00:14:04.230
works pretty well.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:07.700
So some of the best practices
for doing the design process

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:10.980
are one, to have people
assigned to different roles.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.290
So some roles that
work really well

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:15.230
are someone who's in
charge of your timeline.

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:16.855
So are you hitting
those dates that you

00:14:16.855 --> 00:14:17.990
outline on your schedule?

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.507
And regardless, what
can we do right now?

00:14:20.507 --> 00:14:22.340
So if you're waiting
for that mill to arrive

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:24.120
or whatever you've
ordered to arrive,

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.100
it can be really
tempting to be like,

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:26.433
oh, there's nothing to do today.

00:14:26.433 --> 00:14:28.910
It'll be here on Friday and I
won't do anything until then.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:31.430
And that's really dangerous
because time is so tight.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.280
And so figuring out what can we
do right now, even if it's not

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:37.153
on the critical path, just to
make sure we're moving forward,

00:14:37.153 --> 00:14:38.570
and then having
someone else who's

00:14:38.570 --> 00:14:39.987
making sure that
what you're doing

00:14:39.987 --> 00:14:41.720
is actually related
to your goal.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:43.220
Because it can be
easy to get really

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.290
involved in figuring out
the perfect adhesive,

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.690
and doing an adhesive analysis,
when your goal is just

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:49.315
to have a good enough
adhesive, and you

00:14:49.315 --> 00:14:51.530
should use the first one
you found and go with it,

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:53.130
or whatever.

00:14:53.130 --> 00:14:56.300
And then having someone who's
the key person on your team

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:58.972
who's in charge of communicating
with your community partner--

00:14:58.972 --> 00:15:01.430
for this class I'm going to
ask that you try to communicate

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:02.877
with a partner once a week.

00:15:02.877 --> 00:15:04.710
Some of you may have a
hard time doing that.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.200
But you should at
least be trying

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:08.420
to communicate once
a week so that you're

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:09.840
getting regular feedback.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:12.120
And then finally,
documentation--

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.690
making sure that you're
documenting what you do well,

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.190
which you'll really
thank yourselves

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.273
for at the end of the class
when you have to write

00:15:18.273 --> 00:15:19.400
a final report as a team.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:21.350
The more documentation
you do ahead of time,

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:22.220
the easier that'll.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:25.190
Be and then general
recommendations

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:27.350
are failing fast, is one.

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.630
And so it's much
better to figure out

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:31.490
that your idea
doesn't work today

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:33.770
than it is the day
before your presentation.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:35.902
And so the sooner you
can be developing stuff

00:15:35.902 --> 00:15:37.610
so that you can figure
out those failures

00:15:37.610 --> 00:15:39.690
and fixing them, the better.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.830
And so along those lines,
prototyping early and often.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:45.800
And then finally, identifying
any of your deal breakers

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:46.340
immediately.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:48.410
So any physical
impossibilities or things that

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:49.640
are just not going to work--

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:51.410
figuring those out as
soon as possible so

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:56.780
that you don't get into failing
spectacularly, but only failing

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.780
in small areas that
you can recover from.

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:04.100
Oh, I don't know why these
slides are like this.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:05.060
I'm sorry, guys.

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:07.520
I should have run
through it ahead of time.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:09.680
I didn't think it
would be in there.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:12.410
Anyway, so I picked
one of those design

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:14.840
processes to run us
through what we're doing.

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:17.600
And we're basically doing each
stage of the design process

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:20.480
in a week, which is
ridiculously fast.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:25.340
But it's why the pressure
is on automatically,

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:28.160
from today, because this
is all the time we have.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.580
Your final presentation is on
May 7, which is a Saturday.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:32.670
It's in your syllabus.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.360
So if you aren't
free on that day,

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.560
you should talk to me real soon.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:41.810
But we're doing them as a group
with the other D-Lab classes.

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:45.030
And so that's six weeks
total is what we have.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:49.370
And so everything we're
doing is basically,

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:52.400
we have a week for each
stage, which is very rapid.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:56.540
And that really drives what
your project should be.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.750
So as you probably noticed when
I showed last year's projects,

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:03.890
none of them were inventing a
brand-new washing machine, say.

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:06.140
It was making a very small,
incremental improvement

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:06.920
on something.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:08.869
And that's what you
want to do because it's

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:10.430
much better to have
a really awesome

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:12.388
small, incremental
improvement that'll actually

00:17:12.388 --> 00:17:15.500
help your organization and
be of benefit, than to make

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:18.140
very small progress on a really
big project that's not going

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:19.940
to help anyone because
you haven't completed it

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:21.065
by the end of the semester.

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:26.310
So I've created a
Part Two syllabus,

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:29.510
which I'll hand out as soon as
I pick them up from the copier.

00:17:29.510 --> 00:17:34.310
But basically, it has
what we're doing each day,

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:36.080
and then the assignments due.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:39.530
And then below
this, the schedule

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:41.640
is a bunch of details
about each assignment.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.640
So hopefully, everything
will be super clear.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:45.230
But things to know
is that there's

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:47.240
a lot of time this
unscheduled where I'm not

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.530
going to be lecturing at all.

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:50.010
Amit is not going to
be lecturing at all.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.780
It's just time for
your team to work.

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:53.647
That doesn't mean you
should blow off class.

00:17:53.647 --> 00:17:54.980
We're still checking attendance.

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:56.930
We still expect you to be here.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.850
The work time is
pretty valuable.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:01.493
Amit and I will be stopping
by each team every session

00:18:01.493 --> 00:18:03.410
at least once to check
in on how you're doing.

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.810
Your mentors will be stopping
by almost every session

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:07.070
to check in and help you.

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:09.770
So it's a good
time to work where

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:11.500
you know your team
members are available,

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:14.000
where if it's out of class time,
it's harder to get everyone

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.720
together in a group.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.967
And so along those
lines, if you're not

00:18:17.967 --> 00:18:19.550
going to be working
in this classroom,

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:20.810
your team should
leave a note where

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:22.935
you are so that as Amit
and I are doing our rounds,

00:18:22.935 --> 00:18:24.380
we can find you.

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:28.730
So again, we actually do
expect you to be here.

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:32.960
And then second is you're going
to be creating a wiki page.

00:18:32.960 --> 00:18:36.320
There's a wiki link off
of the Stellar website.

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:39.980
And so each team should have
a wiki page where you can

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:41.610
document your design process.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:43.820
So rather than having
physical design books, which

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:45.425
get really challenging
in terms of you

00:18:45.425 --> 00:18:46.940
want them with at
all times, I need

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:49.040
to take them to
grade them, D-Lab

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:50.750
has started doing
wikis so that we

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:53.420
can do grading without
stealing your design notebooks

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:54.300
and stuff like that.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.310
So that's what you should
document everything.

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.550
And it's also useful
for future classes.

00:18:58.550 --> 00:19:00.950
They get to see everything
that you've worked on,

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:03.660
so that can be helpful.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:06.320
And then there will be a
design review on April 22,

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.107
where you will have 10 minutes
to present your project.

00:19:09.107 --> 00:19:10.940
And we'll have people
from outside the class

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:12.740
coming to give feedback.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:15.020
And there, you'll want
to have a prototype.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:17.970
And I'll talk more about
that as it gets closer.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.510
And then the final
presentation's where you'll--

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:22.052
it's this combo
where you're both

00:19:22.052 --> 00:19:23.510
doing a public
presentation that'll

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:25.730
be one minute along
with a poster session.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:27.230
That's what you're
used to if you've

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:29.210
done previous D-Lab
classes in the spring.

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:31.340
And then also doing
a more formal design

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:33.200
review where you'll
leave the poster session

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:37.222
and have a formal design review
in a separate room, as well.

00:19:37.222 --> 00:19:38.930
We used to do those
separately, and we're

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:42.300
trying to combine them
just for efficiency sake.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.610
And then finally, you'll
have a final report,

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:45.950
which is written up well here.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:49.680
And then I'll go through
more as we get closer.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:52.520
So what are we doing now?

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:55.100
You guys need to finalize
what your actual projects are

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:56.370
going to be.

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:59.810
And so what I'd like you to do
is come up with your top end

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:05.960
projects, hopefully at least
three and no more than six,

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:08.510
I would say, would
be a good range.

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:11.420
And do a Pugh chart
with them so that you

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:15.830
can come up with the best--

00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:17.990
figure out what projects
are worth working on.

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:20.780
And you should come up with one
or two are the ones that you

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:23.000
recommend going ahead with.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:25.340
And then at 2:30, which--

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:27.380
wow, I don't
[INAUDIBLE] do micro

00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:29.780
presentations, because
things went long.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:33.830
So probably, we'll
just have you do the--

00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:35.480
just by the end
of class, you need

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:38.540
to hand in to me what are
your top couple of projects

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:40.790
with a little description
that you can email me,

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:44.362
so that everyone can know
what they're working on.

00:20:44.362 --> 00:20:46.820
But hopefully, you're not too
far afield from what you just

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:48.290
presented, so people
have a general idea

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:49.373
of what you're working on.

00:20:49.373 --> 00:20:51.840
So before I let
you go, I just want

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:54.860
to do one last thing,
which is what Pugh

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:56.240
criteria we should be using.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:21:01.360
Because you can have some
variation [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:03.874
you think we should use.

00:21:03.874 --> 00:21:04.750
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:14.290
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so
your budget is about $500.

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:18.490
So you definitely don't want
to be inventing anything that's

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:21.040
going to require $50,000.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:22.218
What else?

00:21:22.218 --> 00:21:26.880
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] So
what if we get funding?

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:28.900
What if our project
is an awesome idea?

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:32.770
Can I look for funding
somewhere else and--

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:35.890
AMY BANZAERT: Yes, but
you have six weeks.

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:42.570
So you're unlikely to get
funding in these six weeks

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:43.180
there.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:48.050
Like if it's $700, let's talk
about what you actually need.

00:21:48.050 --> 00:21:51.100
But most teams had budget
left over last year

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.330
when they had $500.

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:55.570
So I don't think this is
a super high constraint.

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:59.290
It just gives you an
order of magnitude.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:00.670
So what else?

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:02.470
STUDENT: Materials availability.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:06.910
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so I think
a bigger general one is--

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:07.952
do you mean like locally?

00:22:07.952 --> 00:22:08.535
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:22:08.535 --> 00:22:09.130
[INAUDIBLE]

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:13.300
AMY BANZAERT: So then
that would probably

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:17.440
be something more specific
for different examples

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.270
or different projects,
like if you have

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:24.340
12 ideas for your rice husker.

00:22:24.340 --> 00:22:27.730
But I think for here, one
that's along those lines

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:33.020
is just does it
help your community?

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.110
And embedded in that is
if they can't build it,

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.510
it's not going to help them.

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:46.050
But we're looking at very
broad strokes at this level.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:46.800
Any others?

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:48.800
STUDENT: It's going to
be finished in six weeks.

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:56.510
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, so
"si se puede," right?

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:59.420
We want to be able to say that.

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:04.737
So that's pretty important,
to be able to say yes, we can.

00:23:04.737 --> 00:23:06.320
My son is really
into Bob the Builder,

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.180
and that's all I hear
is, can we build it?

00:23:08.180 --> 00:23:08.770
Yes, we can.

00:23:11.570 --> 00:23:13.820
And then one other
that's important to me

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:17.960
is that it's relevant to
the Energy class, which

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:20.270
I think they all are.

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:24.200
But a water project might
be really compelling.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.663
It's not relevant to energy.

00:23:26.663 --> 00:23:28.580
So you want to make sure
that it does actually

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:30.205
fit within the
boundaries of the class,

00:23:30.205 --> 00:23:32.070
but they're pretty
broadly defined.

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:35.495
So these are the
four that I really

00:23:35.495 --> 00:23:37.370
want to make sure you
include, that you might

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:38.580
want to add others, as well.

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:40.910
But every team
should include these.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:44.160
Are there any questions?

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:49.730
So I'm going to grab
the revised syllabi

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:55.610
and the big paper that you can
use to make Pugh charts on.

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:57.900
And that's what you
should be doing.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:02.990
And by the end of the class,
you need to have top projects.

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:04.540
Great.

